{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through ransom payment. This long incarceration shaped his worldview and made him deeply skeptical of reckless military adventures against Muslim forces. He became known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to negotiate truces with Saladin while other nobles preferred constant warfare.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond assumed his title while still young yet his actual rule began only after release from Syrian captivity. The experience taught him the value of patience and strategy over brute force. He understood the complex political landscape of the Levant better than most contemporaries because he lived among his enemies for a decade. This unique perspective allowed him to forge alliances that seemed impossible to other Crusader leaders who viewed all Muslims as irredeemable foes worthy only of destruction.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nHe acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance. Raymond faced intense opposition from the faction led by Guy of Lusignan and Renaud of Châtillon who advocated aggressive policies against Saladin. These rivals accused him of treason or collusion with Muslims simply because he urged caution and diplomacy instead of immediate battle. The internal division within the kingdom weakened its defenses significantly during a time when unity was essential for survival against the growing power of the Ayyubid Sultanate.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nRaymond advised against marching across the arid plateau to relieve the siege of Tiberias but King Guy ignored this counsel and led the army into a trap. The resulting disaster at the Battle of Hattin destroyed the Crusader field army and paved the way for Saladin to capture Jerusalem. Raymond escaped the carnage though his reputation suffered due to accusations of cowardice or betrayal by chroniclers loyal to Guy. He died shortly after the battle possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried to save through prudent statecraft. His life illustrates the tragic conflict between pragmatic diplomacy and religious zealotry that defined the final years of the Crusader states in the Holy Land. Modern historians often view him as one of the most capable leaders of his era whose advice might have prevented total catastrophe if it had been heeded by the fragile monarchy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.7656937e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00319209}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.023412557}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.110941105}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0016136068}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920936e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.009763362}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.031852808}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.028665828}, {"token": " role", "lo